SUMMARY:Digital Filmmaking Basics
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T160000
UID:69cd28bc213814b1b522a087@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Recording a video is one thing\, but making movies is a whole 
 other ballgame. In this class we’ll look at how to use the manual settin
 gs on a digital camera to get that “cinematic” look as well as things 
 such as frame rates\, composition\, lighting\, and aspect ratios.\nCamera 
 equipment will be provided for this class. Number of cameras is limited an
 d participants may be asked to share.\nRegistration is required.\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Leaside\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69cd28bc213814b1b522a087
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/699f392e8089f23c675e1e63"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_events
SUMMARY:Japanese Tea Ceremony
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T150000
UID:699f392e8089f23c675e1e63@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Experience the traditional art of the Japanese tea ceremony.  
 Learn about Japanese culture and the important philosophy behind the tea c
 eremony. This presentation not only offers a demonstration of traditional 
 Matcha preparation using authentic tools\, but zen philosophies which may 
 also apply to daily life. Discover how Matcha is harvested\, its history\,
  how to make/use it\, how to differenciate between different grades\, and 
 how to conserve it.\nReina Sakao is a fourth-generation tea house and Uras
 enke tea instructor who trained at the Urasenke School in Japan where her 
 family owns and operates a tea plantation in Shizuoka. Reina has over 10 y
 ears of experience with the Japanese tea ceremony (first-degree Urasenke t
 ea ceremony instructor).​
LOCATION:Agincourt\, Main Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/699f392e8089f23c675e1e63
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
